Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 14:44:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Preserve none color components through parse, mix and
 serialization

rgb(), hsl() and hwb() with none components eagerly resolved into the
legacy srgb form, which cannot represent none. Computed values came out
as rgb(0, 0, 0), and color-mix carry-forward never saw the missing
components. The serialization rules below follow Blink, as pinned by
WPT color-{valid,computed}-{rgb,hsl,hwb}, color-valid-relative-color,
color-computed-relative-color and color-{valid,computed}-color-mix-function.

Parsing and computed values:

- resolve_to_absolute keeps the none flags. rgb() stays legacy-tagged
  (specified serialization is still rgb(0, 0, 0)) but the flags
  survive; hsl() and hwb() skip the legacy tag entirely when any
  component is none.
- AbsoluteColor::to_css falls back to the modern form for
  none-carrying colors: color(srgb ...) for legacy srgb, and functional
  hsl()/hwb() instead of the srgb conversion. s/l (w/b) carry '%' only
  where the author wrote a percent, tracked per component
  (C1_AS_PERCENT / C2_AS_PERCENT), so computed values round-trip.
  Relative-origin and color-mix results use plain numbers.
- The specified serialization writes legacy-with-zeros for rgb and
  number-form hsl()/hwb().

Relative colors and color-mix:

- Bare channel keywords (r, g, b, ...) carry a none origin channel
  forward in rgb(), hsl(), hwb(), lab() and oklab(); calc() still gets
  0. lch(), oklch() and color() zero them, matching Chrome (the WPT
  rows carry a 'FIXME: Clarify with spec editors').
- hsl() and hwb() relative origins and color-mix items that carry none
  read back as the resolved legacy rgb, with none as 0. The legacy text
  is stamped as the nested color's authored serialization at parse time
  (Color::stamp_nested_legacy_authored); the stored value keeps its
  none flags for channel keywords and mixing.
- mix_many skips the srgb conversion for none-carrying results, which
  would lose them.
- css-values-5 no longer invalidates an all-zero percentage sum: the
  items mix with equal weight and the result's alpha is multiplied by
  zero. Folding pairwise at 50/50 matches Chrome's
  color(srgb 0.25 0.12549 0.5 / 0) for three items.

A color function that stays symbolic (some component is a calc()
resolvable only at computed-value time) echoed every literal component
as authored: rgb(.. 0% 0% / 50%) instead of the canonical
rgb(.. 0 0 / 0.5) that WPT color-valid-* expects. Literal components
now serialize as numbers in the function's reference scale, with the
per-slot clamps the spec applies (rgb 0..255, lab/lch lightness 0..100,
ok* lightness 0..1, chroma and saturation floor 0, alpha 0..1). Hues
drop their angle unit. Relative colors (origin present) keep echoing
the authored form, since WPT color-valid-relative-color asserts the
percent-preserving behavior there.
